LeetCode 234 — Palindrome Linked List
From Brute Force → Most Optimal
All approaches + code + explanation + dry run
1. Problem Understanding

Given the head of a singly linked list, determine whether the linked list is a palindrome.

A palindrome reads the same from left to right and right to left.

Example
1 → 2 → 2 → 1

is a palindrome:

1 2 2 1

But:

1 → 2 → 3

is not:

1 2 3 ≠ 3 2 1

Approach 1 — Brute Force: Convert to Array/List
Idea

A linked list does not allow direct backward traversal.

So:

Copy all values into an ArrayList.
Use two pointers:
one from the beginning
one from the end
Compare them.

Code
class Solution {
    public boolean isPalindrome(ListNode head) {

        ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();

        ListNode curr = head;

        // Store linked list values
        while (curr != null) {
            list.add(curr.val);
            curr = curr.next;
        }

        // Compare from both ends
        int left = 0;
        int right = list.size() - 1;

        while (left < right) {

            if (!list.get(left).equals(list.get(right))) {
                return false;
            }

            left++;
            right--;
        }

        return true;
    }
}

Dry Run

Input:

1 → 2 → 3 → 2 → 1

Convert to array:

[1, 2, 3, 2, 1]

Compare:

left = 0 → 1
right = 4 → 1
1 == 1 ✓

Move:

left = 1
right = 3
2 == 2 ✓

Move:

left = 2
right = 2

Stop.

Return:

true
Complexity
Time  = O(n)
Space = O(n)

Very simple, but extra memory is required.

Approach 2 — Stack
Idea

A stack follows LIFO:

Last In → First Out

For a palindrome, the second half should match the reverse of the first half.

We can:

Push all nodes/values into a stack.
Traverse the list again.
Compare each value with stack.pop().

Code
class Solution {
    public boolean isPalindrome(ListNode head) {

        Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();

        ListNode curr = head;

        // Push all values
        while (curr != null) {
            stack.push(curr.val);
            curr = curr.next;
        }

        // Compare with reverse order
        curr = head;

        while (curr != null) {

            if (curr.val != stack.pop()) {
                return false;
            }

            curr = curr.next;
        }

        return true;
    }
}

