Approach 4 — Optimal + Restore the Linked List ⭐⭐

There is one improvement worth knowing.

The previous approach reverses part of the linked list.

If the problem requires preserving the original list structure, we can:

Find middle.
Reverse second half.
Compare.
Reverse second half again to restore the list.
Code
class Solution {

    public ListNode reverse(ListNode head) {

        ListNode prev = null;
        ListNode curr = head;

        while (curr != null) {

            ListNode next = curr.next;

            curr.next = prev;
            prev = curr;
            curr = next;
        }

        return prev;
    }

    public boolean isPalindrome(ListNode head) {

        if (head == null || head.next == null) {
            return true;
        }

        ListNode slow = head;
        ListNode fast = head;

        // Find second middle
        while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;
        }

        // Reverse second half
        ListNode secondHalf = reverse(slow);

        ListNode firstHalf = head;
        ListNode temp = secondHalf;

        boolean palindrome = true;

        // Compare
        while (temp != null) {

            if (firstHalf.val != temp.val) {
                palindrome = false;
                break;
            }

            firstHalf = firstHalf.next;
            temp = temp.next;
        }

        // Restore second half
        reverse(secondHalf);

        return palindrome;
    }
}

Dry Run of Restoration

Suppose:

1 → 2 → 3 → 2 → 1

After reversing second half:

1 → 2 → 3
          ↑
original first portion

reversed portion:
1 → 2 → 3

After comparison:

1 == 1
2 == 2
3 == 3

Then reverse the second half again.

It goes back to:

1 → 2 → 3 → 2 → 1

So the input structure is preserved.

Approach Comparison
Approach	Technique	Time	Extra Space
1. ArrayList	Copy values + two pointers	O(n)	O(n)
2. Stack	Push all values	O(n)	O(n)
3. Reverse second half ⭐	Slow/Fast + Reverse	O(n)	O(1)
4. Reverse + Restore ⭐⭐	Same + restore list	O(n)	O(1)
Why Approach 3 Is Optimal

We cannot do better than:

O(n)

because we need to inspect the nodes to determine whether they form a palindrome.

And we can achieve:

Space = O(1)

by manipulating the linked list itself.

Therefore:

Time  = O(n)
Space = O(1)

is optimal.

